# ---- Category B: Study tips / how-to (15) ----
POSTS += [
{"slug":"how-to-get-a-plus-sindh-board-exams","title":"How to Get A+ in Sindh Board Exams — A Practical 90-Day Plan","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"A practical 90-day plan to get A+ in Sindh Board exams. Daily routine, subject-wise strategy, past papers and revision — no shortcuts, only method.","kw":"how to get A+ in Sindh Board exams, Sindh Board A+ tips, board exam preparation","intro":"A+ is not about studying 12 hours a day. It is about studying the right things in the right order. This 90-day plan shows you exactly how to get A+ in Sindh Board exams — with a daily routine, a subject-wise strategy, and a revision method that actually works.","points":["First 30 days: finish the syllabus. Read every chapter once and solve every exercise. No past papers yet — first the book, then the paper.","Next 30 days: past papers. Solve the last 5 years of board papers for every subject, timed, with the book closed. Mark yourself honestly.","Last 30 days: revision only. Re-read your notes, re-solve the questions you got wrong, and take one full mock paper per week.","Daily routine: 3 hours in the morning (hard subjects: Math, Physics), 2 hours in the evening (theory: Islamiat, Urdu, English). Morning is for solving, evening is for reading.","The one habit that separates A+ students: they write. They do not just read. Every day, write at least one answer from memory. Writing is what the exam checks."]},
{"slug":"how-to-memorize-quran-surahs-for-exam","title":"How to Memorise Quran Surahs for Exams — 5 Easy Methods","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"5 easy methods to memorise Quran Surahs for school exams. Daily repetition, meaning-first learning and audio revision that actually sticks.","kw":"how to memorise Quran Surahs for exam, Quran memorisation tips students","intro":"Memorising a Surah for the exam is not about repeating it 100 times in one sitting. It is about repeating it a few times every day, with understanding. These five methods help students memorise Surahs faster — and remember them on exam day.","points":["Method 1 — One Ayat a day: learn one Ayat per day, perfectly, then add the next. Ten days, ten Ayaat, permanently remembered.","Method 2 — Meaning first: read the translation before memorising. When you know what the Ayat means, the words stick twice as fast.","Method 3 — Listen and repeat: play the Surah audio 3 times, then recite along. Hearing is half of memorising.","Method 4 — Write it once: after memorising, write the Surah from memory. Writing finds the gaps that speaking hides.","Method 5 — Teach it: recite the Surah to a parent or friend. Teaching is the final test of memorisation."]},
{"slug":"how-to-write-perfect-answer-sindh-board","title":"How to Write the Perfect Answer in Sindh Board Exams","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"Learn the exact answer format Sindh Board examiners want: headings, points, references and presentation that earn full marks.","kw":"how to write perfect answer Sindh Board, board exam answer writing tips","intro":"Two students can know the same answer — one gets full marks, the other gets half. The difference is not knowledge, it is presentation. This guide teaches the exact answer format Sindh Board examiners want.","points":["Start every answer with a definition or introduction line. Never start mid-topic. The first line tells the examiner you understand the question.","Use headings and points, not one long paragraph. Examiners mark points — a wall of text hides your points.","Add one reference in every Islamiat and Pakistan Studies answer: a Quranic Ayat, a Hadith, or a date. References turn a good answer into an A-grade answer.","Leave one line between paragraphs and underline the headings. Neat presentation earns marks before the examiner reads a word.","End every long answer with a conclusion of two lines: what you proved, and what it means for daily life. A conclusion closes the answer properly."]},
{"slug":"how-to-finish-syllabus-in-30-days","title":"How to Finish the Full Syllabus in 30 Days (Without Panic)","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"A realistic 30-day syllabus completion plan for Sindh Board students. Daily targets, subject rotation and the 80/20 rule for exams.","kw":"how to finish syllabus in 30 days, syllabus completion plan Sindh Board","intro":"Thirty days is enough to finish the syllabus — if you stop trying to study everything equally. This plan uses the 80/20 rule: 80% of marks come from 20% of the syllabus. Finish that 20% first, then the rest.","points":["Day 1: list every chapter of every subject. Mark each chapter: easy, medium, hard. You cannot plan what you have not listed.","Days 2-20: two chapters a day — one hard in the morning, one easy in the evening. Hard in the morning because the mind is fresh.","Days 21-25: past papers only. Solve one paper per subject and find your weak chapters. Weak chapters get the last revision.","Days 26-30: revision of weak chapters + notes only. No new topics in the last five days — new topics in the last days create panic.","The rule that makes it work: phone off during study blocks. Two focused hours beat six distracted hours."]},
{"slug":"how-to-study-islamiat-effectively","title":"How to Study Islamiat Effectively — Notes, Memorisation & Answers","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"The best way to study Islamiat: translation-first learning, daily revision, and answer-writing practice that turns knowledge into marks.","kw":"how to study Islamiat effectively, Islamiat study tips","intro":"Islamiat is the easiest subject to score full marks in — and the easiest to lose marks in by writing careless answers. This guide shows how to study Islamiat effectively: the right order, the right notes, and the right answer format.","points":["Always study in this order: translation, then tashreeh, then questions. Translation first — without it, the tashreeh has no base.","Make a one-page summary per chapter: 5 key points, 2 references, 1 example. One page per chapter is all you need for revision.","Memorise the Arabic of key Ayaat and Ahadith. Even two lines of Arabic in an answer show the examiner you studied from the source.","Practise writing one long answer per day from memory. Islamiat is a writing subject — reading alone never prepares you for it.","Revise Islamiat every Friday. Weekly revision keeps the Ayaat fresh — monthly revision lets them fade."]},
{"slug":"how-to-improve-handwriting-board-exams","title":"How to Improve Handwriting for Board Exams — 10-Minute Daily Drill","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"Improve handwriting for board exams with a 10-minute daily drill. Legible writing earns marks — messy writing loses them.","kw":"how to improve handwriting board exams, handwriting tips students","intro":"Examiners are human. If they cannot read your answer, they cannot give you marks — even if the answer is correct. This 10-minute daily drill improves handwriting fast, without any special copybooks.","points":["Write slowly for 10 minutes a day. Speed comes after clarity. Fast and unreadable is worse than slow and clear.","Keep letters the same size and words on the line. The two biggest handwriting problems are floating words and mixed sizes.","Leave a finger-width gap between words. Crowded words are the top reason examiners misread answers.","Use the same pen for the whole paper. Changing pens changes your handwriting rhythm.","Practise by copying one paragraph from your textbook daily. Copying trains the hand better than free writing."]},
{"slug":"how-to-manage-time-during-exams","title":"How to Manage Time During Exams — The 3-Hour Paper Plan","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"A simple time plan for 3-hour board papers: how long per section, when to skip, and how to finish with 15 minutes to spare.","kw":"how to manage time during exams, exam time management tips","intro":"Most students who fail to finish the paper know the answers — they just run out of time. This 3-hour paper plan fixes that: a fixed time per section, a rule for hard questions, and 15 minutes saved for checking.","points":["Before writing: spend 5 minutes reading the whole paper and marking the questions you will attempt. Five minutes of planning saves thirty minutes of panic.","Divide the paper by marks: a 10-mark question gets 20 minutes, a 5-mark question gets 10 minutes. Time follows marks, not feelings.","If a question takes more than its time, skip it and move on. A skipped question costs one question; a stuck question costs the whole paper.","Keep 15 minutes at the end for checking: spelling, question numbers, and missed parts. Most students lose marks on parts they forgot to answer.","Never leave a question blank. A half answer can earn half marks; a blank earns zero."]},
{"slug":"how-to-prepare-mcqs-sindh-board","title":"How to Prepare MCQs for Sindh Board — Score 100% in Objective Section","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"MCQ preparation for Sindh Board: how to practise, eliminate wrong options, and avoid the traps that cost marks.","kw":"how to prepare MCQs Sindh Board, MCQ tips board exams","intro":"MCQs look easy, but one careless minute can cost five marks. This guide shows how to prepare MCQs for Sindh Board — and how to solve them in the exam without falling into the usual traps.","points":["Prepare MCQs from the textbook, not from guides. Board MCQs come from textbook lines — read every chapter's bold words and definitions.","Practise 50 MCQs per subject per week from past papers. Past paper MCQs repeat more than any other question type.","In the exam, read all four options before choosing. The first option that looks right is often the trap.","Eliminate two wrong options first, then choose between the remaining two. Elimination turns guessing into reasoning.","Never change your first answer unless you are sure. Research shows the first answer is right more often than the changed one."]},
{"slug":"how-to-revise-before-exam-night","title":"How to Revise the Night Before the Exam — Without Fear","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"The night-before-exam revision method: what to read, what to skip, when to sleep, and how to walk in calm.","kw":"how to revise before exam night, night before exam tips","intro":"The night before the exam is not for learning new things. It is for making what you already know fresh and calm. This method shows exactly what to revise the night before — and when to stop and sleep.","points":["Revise only your one-page summaries and the questions you got wrong before. New topics the night before create fear, not marks.","Stop studying by 10 pm. A rested brain remembers more than a tired brain that studied till 2 am.","Pack everything before sleeping: admit card, pens, geometry box, water. Morning panic comes from unprepared bags.","Read one easy chapter before bed — something you know well. Ending the day with success builds confidence.","In the morning, eat breakfast and reach the centre early. A calm start is worth five marks."]},
{"slug":"how-to-make-notes-from-stbb-books","title":"How to Make Notes from STBB Books — The One-Page Method","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"Make your own notes from STBB books with the one-page method: headings, points, references and examples on a single page per chapter.","kw":"how to make notes from STBB books, notes making method students","intro":"Good notes are not a copy of the book — they are the book squeezed into one page. This one-page method shows how to make notes from STBB books that you can actually revise the night before the exam.","points":["One chapter = one page. If your notes are longer than the chapter, they are not notes — they are a second book.","Use this layout: title at top, 5 key points in the middle, 2 references at the bottom, 1 example in the corner. Same layout every time.","Write notes in your own words. Copied notes are forgotten; your own words are remembered.","Use colours sparingly: one colour for headings, one for references. Too many colours turn notes into decoration.","Revise from your notes, not from the book. If your notes cannot answer a past paper question, add one line — then they can."]},
{"slug":"how-to-learn-urdu-grammar-fast","title":"How to Learn Urdu Grammar Fast — 15 Minutes a Day","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"Learn Urdu grammar fast with 15 minutes daily practice. Ism, fail, harf, wahid-jama and jumla — the grammar that actually comes in exams.","kw":"how to learn Urdu grammar fast, Urdu grammar tips","intro":"Urdu grammar feels hard because students try to learn it all at once. Fifteen minutes a day, one topic at a time, makes it easy. This plan covers the grammar that actually comes in Sindh Board exams.","points":["Week 1: Ism, Fail, Harf. Learn one per day with 5 examples each. Three days, three concepts, fifteen examples.","Week 2: Wahid-Jama and Muzakkar-Muannas. Make a table of 20 words and convert each. Tables are faster than rules.","Week 3: Jumla — Ismia and Failia. Write 5 of each daily from your textbook lessons.","Week 4: Past paper grammar questions only. Grammar from past papers is the grammar that comes again.","The golden rule: never learn a rule without an example. One example teaches more than ten rules."]},
{"slug":"how-to-solve-math-word-problems","title":"How to Solve Math Word Problems — A 4-Step Method That Works","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"Solve any Math word problem with 4 steps: understand, plan, solve, check. With worked examples from STBB books.","kw":"how to solve Math word problems, word problems method","intro":"Word problems scare students because they mix reading and maths. This 4-step method separates the two — first read, then solve — and makes every word problem beatable.","points":["Step 1 — Understand: read the problem twice. Underline the numbers and the question. What is given? What is asked?","Step 2 — Plan: choose the operation or formula. If it says 'total', it is addition. If it says 'left', it is subtraction. Words hint the operation.","Step 3 — Solve: write the equation, then solve step by step. One step per line — never do two steps in your head.","Step 4 — Check: put the answer back in the problem. Does it make sense? If a boy's age comes out as 200, the answer is wrong."]},
{"slug":"how-to-remember-history-dates","title":"How to Remember History Dates — Timeline, Story & Link Method","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"Remember history dates easily with the timeline, story and link method. No rote memorisation — just stories that stick.","kw":"how to remember history dates, history dates memorisation","intro":"History dates feel impossible because students try to memorise numbers alone. Numbers alone are forgotten. Numbers inside a story are remembered. This method turns dates into stories.","points":["Draw a timeline for every chapter. Put the events in order on one line. Order is remembered before dates.","Link each date to a story: 1947 — Pakistan's birth. 1971 — separation. Stories hold dates like hooks hold coats.","Group dates by decade: 1940s, 1950s, 1960s. Groups are easier to recall than a random list.","Test yourself backwards: from the last event to the first. Backwards testing proves real memory, not just sequence memory.","Revise the timeline every Sunday. Weekly revision keeps the whole timeline alive."]},
{"slug":"how-to-score-full-marks-islamiat","title":"How to Score Full Marks in Islamiat — The Examiner's Checklist","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"The examiner's checklist for full marks in Islamiat: translation, references, structure, examples and presentation.","kw":"how to score full marks Islamiat, Islamiat full marks tips","intro":"Full marks in Islamiat are not luck — they follow a checklist. Examiners look for five things in every answer. This guide gives you that checklist so you can write answers that leave nothing for the examiner to cut.","points":["Checklist item 1: correct translation of every Ayat and Hadith. Translation is the base mark — get it right first.","Checklist item 2: one reference per answer. A Quranic Ayat or a Hadith reference proves the answer comes from the source.","Checklist item 3: structure — definition, explanation, example, conclusion. Four parts, every long answer.","Checklist item 4: one daily-life example. Islamiat is about life — an answer without a life example feels incomplete.","Checklist item 5: neat presentation — headings underlined, one line between paragraphs, Arabic written correctly."]},
{"slug":"how-to-avoid-common-exam-mistakes","title":"10 Common Exam Mistakes That Cost Marks (And How to Avoid Them)","cat":"Study Tips","desc":"The 10 most common exam mistakes in Sindh Board — wrong question numbers, blank questions, unreadable writing — and how to avoid each one.","kw":"common exam mistakes, exam mistakes to avoid Sindh Board","intro":"Every year, thousands of marks are lost not to ignorance but to careless mistakes. These ten mistakes are the most common in Sindh Board exams — and every one of them is avoidable.","points":["Mistake 1: wrong question number. Always write the question number before the answer — and check it twice.","Mistake 2: leaving questions blank. A half answer earns half marks; a blank earns zero. Always write something.","Mistake 3: answering only part of the question. Read the question twice — many questions have two parts.","Mistake 4: unreadable handwriting. If the examiner cannot read it, it is wrong — even if it is right.","Mistake 5: no headings in long answers. Headings guide the examiner's eyes to your points.","Mistake 6: forgetting units in Math and Science. A number without a unit is an incomplete answer.","Mistake 7: spending too long on one question. Time follows marks — a 5-mark question never deserves 30 minutes.","Mistake 8: not reading the paper first. Five minutes of reading saves thirty minutes of regret.","Mistake 9: changing answers at the last minute. Your first answer is usually right — change it only with proof.","Mistake 10: skipping revision time. Save 15 minutes to check — most lost marks are found in checking."]},
]

# ---- Category C: Board news / syllabus (10) ----
POSTS += [
{"slug":"sindh-board-class-9-syllabus-2026","title":"Sindh Board Class 9 Syllabus 2026 — All Subjects (Complete Guide)","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"Complete Sindh Board Class 9 syllabus 2026 for all subjects. STBB books, paper pattern and chapter list for Science and Computer Science groups.","kw":"Sindh Board Class 9 syllabus 2026, Class 9 syllabus Sindh Board","intro":"Class 9 is the first board class, and students who start with the full syllabus in front of them always do better than those who study blind. This guide gives the complete Sindh Board Class 9 syllabus 2026 — every subject, every book, and the paper pattern for each.","points":["Compulsory subjects: Urdu, English, Islamiat, Pakistan Studies, Mathematics. Every student takes these five, whichever group they choose.","Science group: Physics, Chemistry, Biology. Computer Science group: Physics, Chemistry, Computer Science. Choose the group by interest, not by friends.","Islamiat syllabus: Quran (translation + tashreeh of selected Ayaat), Hadith (selected Ahadith), Beliefs, Worship, Seerat, Akhlaq, and Muslim Personalities.","Urdu and English syllabus: lessons, poems, grammar, comprehension, letters and essays. Grammar and writing together carry nearly half the marks.","The smartest start: download the STBB books, list every chapter, and tick chapters off as you finish them. A ticked list is the best motivation."]},
{"slug":"sindh-board-class-10-syllabus-2026","title":"Sindh Board Class 10 Syllabus 2026 — All Subjects (Complete Guide)","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"Complete Sindh Board Class 10 syllabus 2026. All subjects, STBB books and paper patterns for Science and Computer Science groups.","kw":"Sindh Board Class 10 syllabus 2026, Class 10 syllabus Sindh Board","intro":"Class 10 is the Matric year — the syllabus you finish here decides your board result. This guide lists the complete Sindh Board Class 10 syllabus 2026, subject by subject, with the paper pattern and the chapters that carry the most marks.","points":["Compulsory subjects: Urdu, English, Islamiat, Pakistan Studies, Mathematics. These five are common to both groups.","Science group: Physics, Chemistry, Biology. Computer Science group: Physics, Chemistry, Computer Science.","Islamiat in Class 10 goes deeper than Class 9: more Ayaat, more Ahadith, and longer Seerat and Akhlaq questions. Start Islamiat early — it is the easiest A+ subject.","Physics and Chemistry papers split into theory and numericals. Prepare both — students who only read theory fail the numericals.","The 10th syllabus repeats Class 9 concepts at a higher level. If a Class 9 chapter is weak, revise it before starting the Class 10 version."]},
{"slug":"sindh-board-exam-dates-2026-class-9-10","title":"Sindh Board Exam Dates 2026 — Class 9 & 10 (Expected Schedule)","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"Expected Sindh Board exam dates 2026 for Class 9 and 10. SSC Part I & II schedule for Karachi, Hyderabad, Sukkur, Larkana and Mirpurkhas boards.","kw":"Sindh Board exam dates 2026 Class 9 10, SSC exam dates Sindh 2026","intro":"Every year, students ask the same question: when will the exams start? This page gives the expected Sindh Board exam dates 2026 for Class 9 and 10, based on the official board calendar pattern — plus how to plan your preparation around them.","points":["Class 10 (SSC Part II) exams usually start first, in March or April, followed by Class 9 (SSC Part I). The gap between the two is usually two to three weeks.","Each board — Karachi, Hyderabad, Sukkur, Larkana, Mirpurkhas — announces its own date sheet. Check your own board's website; dates differ by a few days.","Date sheets usually arrive 3-4 weeks before the first paper. That is the time to switch from learning to revision — no new topics after the date sheet.","Plan backwards from the expected first paper: 90 days before, finish the syllabus; 30 days before, start past papers; 7 days before, revise only notes.","Always confirm dates on your board's official website. This page gives the expected schedule; the board's notice is final."]},
{"slug":"sindh-board-paper-pattern-class-9-10","title":"Sindh Board Paper Pattern Class 9 & 10 — Marks, Sections & MCQs","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"Sindh Board paper pattern for Class 9 and 10. Section-wise marks, MCQs, short and long questions for every subject.","kw":"Sindh Board paper pattern Class 9 10, SSC paper pattern Sindh","intro":"Students who know the paper pattern never get surprised in the exam hall. This guide explains the Sindh Board paper pattern for Class 9 and 10 — how many marks, how many sections, and how much time each section deserves.","points":["Most papers have three sections: Section A — MCQs (objective), Section B — Short Questions, Section C — Long Questions. MCQs are usually 10-20% of the paper.","MCQs: one mark each, no negative marking. Attempt all — a guess can earn a mark, a blank never does.","Short questions: 2-4 marks each. Answer in 3-5 lines. Short means short — long answers here waste time.","Long questions: 7-10 marks each. Answer with headings, points and a conclusion. Long questions carry the most marks per question.","Time rule: spend marks-proportional time. A 10-mark question deserves twice the time of a 5-mark question — never more."]},
{"slug":"sindh-board-class-8-syllabus-2026","title":"Sindh Board Class 8 Syllabus 2026 — All Subjects (STBB Books)","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"Complete Sindh Board Class 8 syllabus 2026. All STBB books and chapter lists for Class 8 students and teachers.","kw":"Sindh Board Class 8 syllabus 2026, Class 8 syllabus Sindh Board","intro":"Class 8 is the last year before board classes — the year to build strong foundations. This guide lists the complete Sindh Board Class 8 syllabus 2026 with STBB books for every subject.","points":["Core subjects: Sindhi, Urdu, English, Mathematics, Science, Islamiat, Social Studies. Plus Arabic in many schools.","Islamiat syllabus: Quran (Nazra + Hifz), Beliefs and Worship, Seerat, Akhlaq, and Muslim Personalities.","Science in Class 8 introduces Physics, Chemistry and Biology concepts. The students who master Class 8 Science find Class 9 Science easy.","Social Studies covers geography, history and civics — the base for Class 9-10 Pakistan Studies.","Class 8 exams are school-based, but study them like board exams. The habits you build in Class 8 decide your Class 9 result."]},
{"slug":"stbb-new-books-2026-changes","title":"STBB New Books 2026 — What Changed in the Sindh Textbooks?","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"What changed in STBB new books 2026? Updated chapters, new exercises and what students and teachers need to know.","kw":"STBB new books 2026, Sindh textbook changes 2026","intro":"Every few years, the Sindh Textbook Board updates its books — new chapters, revised exercises, and sometimes a new paper pattern. This page summarises the STBB new books 2026 changes and what they mean for students.","points":["Updated exercises: several chapters now have more application-based questions — questions that ask 'what would you do?' instead of only 'what is it?'","New chapters added in some subjects reflect current topics. Always check your book's table of contents against last year's — do not study from old guides alone.","Some books have new diagrams and maps. Diagrams from the new book are the ones the examiner expects — learn them, not old ones.","Solved exercises on this site follow the latest STBB books. If your book differs, check the chapter title, not the chapter number — numbers sometimes shift.","Teachers: compare the old and new book side by side before teaching. One changed exercise can change the whole lesson plan."]},
{"slug":"bise-hyderabad-karachi-sukkur-board-dates","title":"BISE Hyderabad, Karachi, Sukkur, Larkana & Mirpurkhas — Board Exam Guide","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"Guide to Sindh's five boards: BISE Hyderabad, Karachi, Sukkur, Larkana and Mirpurkhas. Date sheets, roll number slips and result checking.","kw":"BISE Hyderabad Karachi Sukkur board dates, Sindh boards exam guide","intro":"Sindh has five boards of intermediate and secondary education: Karachi, Hyderabad, Sukkur, Larkana and Mirpurkhas. Each board runs its own exams, date sheets and results. This guide explains how all five work — and where to find your board's notices.","points":["Your board is decided by your school's district. Karachi schools fall under BSEK Karachi; Hyderabad district under BISE Hyderabad, and so on.","Date sheets appear on each board's official website and in schools. The date sheet in your school is the final one — follow it.","Roll number slips come through your school for regular students, and by post or online for private students. Check your slip's centre and timing a day early.","Results are announced on each board's website. Keep your roll number safe — you need it to check the result.","If you find a mistake in your slip (name, subjects, photo), report it to your school immediately. Mistakes fixed early cost nothing; mistakes fixed late cost months."]},
{"slug":"sindh-board-result-2026-check-online","title":"Sindh Board Result 2026 — How to Check Online (All Boards)","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"How to check Sindh Board result 2026 online for Class 9, 10, 11, 12. Step-by-step for Karachi, Hyderabad, Sukkur, Larkana, Mirpurkhas.","kw":"Sindh Board result 2026 check online, BISE result Sindh","intro":"Result day is stressful enough without confusion about where to check. This guide shows how to check your Sindh Board result 2026 online — for Class 9, 10, 11 and 12 — on any of Sindh's five boards.","points":["Step 1: go to your own board's official website. Each board has its own result page — the wrong board's site will not show your result.","Step 2: select your class (SSC Part I, SSC Part II, HSSC Part I, HSSC Part II) and enter your roll number exactly as printed on your slip.","Step 3: if the website is slow on result day, wait and retry. Thousands of students check at once — slowness is normal, not a failed result.","Step 4: save or print your result card. The online result is provisional — the original marksheet comes from your school later.","If your result shows 'absent' or a subject is missing, contact your school the same day. Early complaints are resolved fastest."]},
{"slug":"stbb-free-textbooks-how-to-get","title":"STBB Free Textbooks — How to Get Free Sindh Board Books","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"How to get STBB free textbooks in Sindh. Government free book scheme, school distribution and free PDF downloads.","kw":"STBB free textbooks, free Sindh Board books, Sindh free books scheme","intro":"The Sindh government provides free STBB textbooks to students of government schools. This guide explains how the free book scheme works, how to get your books, and where to download free PDFs if your books are delayed.","points":["Government school students receive free STBB books at the start of the session, distributed through their school. Ask your class teacher if books are delayed.","Private school students usually buy books from the market. Check the STBB book list for your class before buying — buy the latest edition.","If a book is out of stock, use the free official PDF from the STBB eBooks portal while you wait. Never let a missing book stop your studies.","Keep your books covered and clean. Free books are government property — a well-kept book lasts the full year and can help the next student.","Lost a book? Ask your school first — many schools keep spare copies. Otherwise, the STBB portal PDF is your free backup."]},
{"slug":"class-9-10-grading-system-sindh-board","title":"Class 9 & 10 Grading System — Sindh Board Marks, Grades & GPA","cat":"Board & Syllabus","desc":"Sindh Board Class 9 and 10 grading system explained. Marks, grades, GPA and division — how your Matric result is calculated.","kw":"Class 9 10 grading system Sindh Board, Sindh Board grades GPA","intro":"Your Matric result is not just a number — it is a grade, a division, and sometimes a GPA. This guide explains the Sindh Board Class 9 and 10 grading system in plain language, so you know exactly what your marks mean.","points":["Each subject carries marks as per the paper pattern — usually 75 or 100. Your total is the sum of all subjects across Class 9 and 10.","Grades run from A+ down to E. A+ is 80% and above; A is 70-79%; B is 60-69%; C is 50-59%; D is 40-49%; below 40% is fail in most subjects.","Division: First Division is 60% and above; Second Division is 45-59%; Third Division is 33-44%. Divisions matter for college admission.","GPA (where used) is calculated from grade points, not raw marks. An A+ in a subject gives the highest grade point.","The Matric result combines Class 9 and Class 10. A weak Class 9 can be recovered in Class 10 — but only if you treat Class 10 as a full year, not a half year."]},
]
